"""Authored C and C++ extensions: compilation, representation, and ownership."""
from build import S, lesson as L

def c():
 return [
 L('build','From source files to an executable',
 'C separates preprocessing, compilation, and linking. A header declares an interface; an object file contains compiled definitions. Compile every implementation file and link the resulting objects once. Read diagnostics by stage: a syntax error differs from a missing definition at link time. The commands here use GCC with portable C17; compiler flags are tool-specific. Warnings help catch suspicious code but a successful compilation is not proof of valid memory access.',
 S('$ gcc -std=c17 -Wall -Wextra ~-c~ main.c','Produces main.o without linking an executable','Runs main.c|Produces a shared library','-c stops before linking, producing an object file.','-E|-S','What artifact does this command request?'),
 S('$ gcc main.o helper.o ~-o app~','Links the two objects into app','Creates a header file|Runs the tests','The linker combines definitions into the named executable.','-c|-E','What does this command do?'),
 S('/* main.c declares int size(void), but size is never defined. */\n$ gcc main.c ~-o app~','Link error: missing definition of size when main calls it','Guaranteed syntax error|Returns zero at runtime','A declaration lets a call compile; linking still needs a definition.','-c|-E','Assume main calls size(): which stage fails?'),
 S('$ gcc -std=c17 ~-Wall -Wextra~ main.c -o app','Enables additional compiler diagnostics','Makes all undefined behavior safe|Executes the binary','Warnings reveal suspicious constructs; they cannot prove correctness.','-w|-E','What do these flags provide?')),
 L('integer-model','Integer representation and conversions',
 'C integer types have ranges, not arbitrary precision. sizeof(char) is one C byte; CHAR_BIT tells how many bits it contains. Do not assume int is always 32 bits. Unsigned arithmetic wraps, but signed overflow is undefined behavior. Integer promotion and signed/unsigned conversions happen before arithmetic and comparisons. A cast before division changes the operation; a cast after division cannot recover its discarded fraction.',
 S('printf("%zu", ~sizeof(char)~);','1','8|Platform-dependent','sizeof measures C bytes, and char occupies exactly one.','sizeof(int)|sizeof(double)'),
 S('printf("%d", -1 ~<~ 1u);','0','1|-1','The signed -1 converts to unsigned int for this comparison and becomes a large value.','>|!='),
 S('printf("%.1f", ~(double)5~ / 2);','2.5','2.0|3.0','Converting an operand before division selects floating-point division.','5|(double)(5/2)'),
 S('int x=INT_MAX; printf("%d", ~x+1~);','Undefined behavior','Always INT_MIN|Always zero','Signed overflow has no portable result; check the boundary before adding.','x|0','What does signed overflow imply?')),
 L('buffers','Text buffers, capacity, and bounded formatting',
 'A buffer has a capacity; its current string length can be smaller. Reserve space for the terminating zero, and pass capacity alongside pointers. snprintf returns the length it would have written, excluding the terminator: a nonnegative result at least as large as capacity means truncation. Check negative results separately. memcpy copies bytes and requires nonoverlapping regions; memmove permits overlap. These contracts matter more than guessing what an invalid access might print.',
 S('char b[4]; int needed=snprintf(b,sizeof b,"%s","${word}"); printf("%d", ~needed >= (int)sizeof b~);','1','0|-1','Every sampled word has at least five bytes, so the four-byte buffer truncates.','needed < 0|needed == 0'),
 S('char b[4]; snprintf(b,sizeof b,"%s","${word}"); printf("%zu", ~strlen(b)~);','3','4|${length}','Three bytes of text and one terminator fit in this buffer.','sizeof b|0UL'),
 S('char b[]="${word}"; ~memmove~(b,b+1,strlen(b)); printf("%s",b);','${tail}','${word}|Empty string','memmove supports overlap; this count includes the source terminator after shifting left.','memcpy|memset'),
 S('char b[]="${word}"; printf("%d", b[sizeof b-1] ~==~ \'\\0\');','1','0|Undefined behavior','String initialization places the zero terminator after the text.','!=|>')),
 L('storage','Storage duration and pointer contracts',
 'A block-local automatic object ends when its block exits. A static local retains its value across calls. Returning a pointer to a local automatic object does not extend its lifetime; returning a value or using caller-owned storage avoids the dangling pointer. A const pointer and a pointer to const constrain different operations. An array parameter is adjusted to a pointer, so pass its count explicitly instead of reconstructing it with sizeof.',
 S('int next(void){static int n=0; return ~++n~;}\n/* in main */ next(); printf("%d",next());','2','1|0','The static local is initialized once and retains the first increment.','n|n+1'),
 S('int a=${n},b=${m}; int *const p=&a; ~*p=b~; printf("%d",a);','${m}','${n}|Compile error','The pointer target can change even though p cannot be reseated.','p=&b|*p=0'),
 S('size_t count(int a[],size_t n){return ~n~;}\n/* in main */ int a[3]={0}; printf("%zu",count(a,3));','3','1|Platform-dependent','The caller supplies the element count; a is a pointer inside the function.','sizeof(a)|sizeof(a[0])'),
 S('int value(void){int local=${n}; return ~local~;}\n/* in main */ printf("%d",value());','${n}','An address|Undefined behavior','Returning the integer copies its value before the local lifetime ends.','&local|0')),
 L('bits','Unsigned masks and byte-level inspection',
 'Masks express flags in unsigned integers. Bitwise & tests selected bits, | sets them, and ^ toggles them; logical operators instead produce truth values and can short-circuit. Keep shift counts within the type width and use unsigned operands. An object representation may be inspected through unsigned char; its byte order is platform-dependent. These are representation-reading exercises, not algorithms or hardware-specific register programming.',
 S('unsigned flags=5u; printf("%u", flags ~&~ 1u);','1','5|4','Binary 101 AND 001 keeps only the low bit.','^|+'),
 S('unsigned flags=5u; printf("%u", flags ~^~ 1u);','4','1|6','XOR toggles the selected low bit off.','&|+'),
 S('printf("%u", 1u ~<<~ 3);','8','0|3','Shifting unsigned one by three produces eight; this count fits every unsigned int.','>>|+'),
 S('int value=${n}; const ~unsigned char~ *bytes=(const unsigned char *)&value;','May inspect the object representation byte by byte','Guarantees little-endian order|Always exposes four bytes','Character-type access can inspect representations, but size and byte order remain platform-dependent.','double|long','What is this pointer suitable for?')),
 L('cli','Reading a C command-line boundary',
 'main receives argc and argv; argv[0] usually names the executable, and argv[argc] is a null pointer. Check counts before indexing. strtol exposes where parsing stopped; also check range and errno when accepting arbitrary input. Keep diagnostics on stderr and return a failure status when the operation fails. Assertions describe programmer invariants and may be compiled out with NDEBUG; do not use them as the only check on user input.',
 S('/* Inside main(int argc,char **argv); invocation: app ${word} */\nprintf("%s",argv[~1~]);','${word}','app|Undefined behavior','With this invocation the first user argument is argv[1].','0|argc','Given the stated invocation, what is printed?'),
 S('char *end; long n=strtol("${n}x",&end,10); printf("%c",~*end~);','x','${n}|0','strtol stops at x, allowing the caller to reject a trailing suffix.','\'0\'|\'y\''),
 S('~fprintf~(stderr,"invalid ${word}\\n");','Writes a diagnostic to stderr','Writes to stdout|Sets an exit status','The explicit stream keeps diagnostics separate from normal output.','printf|sprintf','Where is the message sent?'),
 S('/* At the end of main after an input error */\nreturn ~EXIT_FAILURE~;','Reports an unsuccessful process exit','Always exits successfully|Automatically retries','EXIT_FAILURE is the portable failure status macro.','EXIT_SUCCESS|0','What does this return communicate?'))]

def cpp():
 return [
 L('build','C++ translation units and build diagnostics',
 'A C++ project compiles source files and links their definitions. Headers expose declarations, while implementation files provide definitions. Use the C++ compiler driver to link the standard C++ library. The commands here use GCC and C++20. Read compiler errors separately from linker errors, and distinguish both from runtime exceptions. A successful build is only the start of validation; assertions can check invariants during development.',
 S('$ g++ ~-std=c++20~ main.cpp -o app','Selects C++20 language rules','Selects Python syntax|Runs the application','The standard flag selects the language dialect for this compilation.','-std=c17|-E','What does the flag configure?'),
 S('$ g++ ~-c~ widget.cpp','Produces an object file without linking','Runs widget.cpp|Publishes a package','-c produces widget.o for a later link step.','-E|-S','What does the command produce?'),
 S('$ g++ main.o widget.o ~-o app~','Links an executable named app','Checks only formatting|Creates a header','Both compiled translation units participate in this link.','-c|-E','What does this step do?'),
 S('~static_assert~(sizeof(char)==1);','Checks the invariant at compile time','Runs only when main calls it|Checks user input at runtime','static_assert requires a compile-time boolean condition.','assert|if','When is this invariant checked?')),
 L('initialization','Initialization, auto, and const references',
 'Prefer initializing an object at its declaration. Brace initialization rejects narrowing conversions such as a fractional double to int. auto deduces a value type and usually drops a reference; auto& preserves aliasing. const auto& gives a read-only view without copying. Read a declaration as a contract about conversion and ownership, rather than assuming that every assignment shares an object as in Python.',
 S('int n~{2.5}~; std::cout<<n;','Compile error: narrowing conversion','2|3','List initialization rejects this narrowing double-to-int conversion.','{2}|(2.5)','What does the compiler report?'),
 S('int n=${n}; ~auto~ copy=n; ++copy; std::cout<<n;','${n}','${next}|Compile error','auto creates an independent integer value here.','auto&|const auto&'),
 S('int n=${n}; ~auto&~ alias=n; ++alias; std::cout<<n;','${next}','${n}|Compile error','The reference aliases n, so incrementing it changes n.','auto|const auto&'),
 S('std::string text="${word}"; ~const auto&~ view=text; std::cout<<view.size();','${length}','0|Compile error','A const reference reads the existing string without copying it.','auto*|int&')),
 L('value-semantics','Moves, ownership transfer, and the rule of zero',
 'Value types such as string copy their contents, whereas unique_ptr is move-only. std::move is a cast that enables a move-aware operation; it does not itself move or destroy anything. A moved-from standard string remains valid but its contents are unspecified. A moved-from unique_ptr is empty. Prefer composing resource-owning standard types so compiler-generated special members manage cleanup: this is the rule of zero.',
 S('auto a=std::make_unique<int>(${n}); auto b=~a~;','Compile error: unique_ptr cannot be copied','Both pointers own independent integers|a becomes empty','Exclusive ownership cannot be duplicated by copying unique_ptr.','std::move(a)|std::make_unique<int>(0)','What happens during initialization of b?'),
 S('auto a=std::make_unique<int>(${n}); auto b=~std::move(a)~; std::cout<<*b;','${n}','0|Compile error','Move construction transfers ownership into b.','a|nullptr'),
 S('auto a=std::make_unique<int>(${n}); auto b=std::move(a); std::cout<<(a ~==~ nullptr);','1','0|Compile error','unique_ptr specifies that the moved-from pointer is empty.','!=|>'),
 S('struct Note {~std::string~ text;};\n// in main\nNote a{"${word}"}; auto b=a; b.text[0]=\'X\'; std::cout<<a.text;','${word}','X${tail}|Compile error','The generated copy operation uses string value semantics; no manual destructor is needed.','char*|std::string_view')),
 L('views','Non-owning views and lifetime boundaries',
 'string_view and span describe existing storage without owning it. Their cheap copies do not extend the lifetime of the underlying object. span carries an element count, unlike a raw pointer. Mutating a span of mutable elements changes the original data. A view of a temporary string can dangle immediately; keep an owner alive or return an owning value when constructing new text.',
 S('std::string owner="${word}"; std::string_view view=owner; std::cout<<view.~substr(0,1)~;','${first}','${word}|Empty string','The substring view refers to the first character of the live owner.','substr(1)|substr(0)'),
 S('int values[]={${n},${m}}; std::span<int> view=values; view[0]~+=1~; std::cout<<values[0];','${next}','${n}|0','span borrows the array; modifying its element updates the array.','+=0|=0'),
 S('int values[]={${n},${m}}; std::span<const int> view=values; std::cout<<view.~size()~;','2','${n}|${m}','span retains the number of elements in its view.','front()|back()'),
 S('std::string_view view=~std::string("${word}")~; std::cout<<view;','Undefined behavior: view refers to destroyed storage','Always ${word}|Compile error','The temporary string dies at the first semicolon; the view does not keep it alive.','"${word}"|"other"','Why is the output invalid?')),
 L('variants','Sum types and constrained generic contracts',
 'Use enum class for scoped states and variant for alternatives with different payloads. get_if checks the active variant without throwing; a raw get of the wrong alternative throws bad_variant_access. Concepts express the operations a generic interface accepts and provide earlier diagnostics. These tools let APIs state their possible values and requirements instead of relying on informal comments or unsafe casts.',
 S('std::variant<int,std::string> value=${n}; auto p=std::~get_if<int>~(&value); std::cout<<*p;','${n}','0|Compile error','get_if returns a pointer because int is the active alternative.','get_if<std::string>|get<int>'),
 S('std::variant<int,std::string> value="${word}"; std::cout<<std::~get<int>~(value);','Throws std::bad_variant_access','0|Compile error','The string alternative is active, so requesting int throws.','get<std::string>|visit','What happens at runtime?'),
 S('template<std::~integral~ T> T next(T n){return n+1;}\n// in main\nstd::cout<<next(${n});','${next}','${n}|Compile error','integral constrains T to integer types.','floating_point|same_as'),
 S('enum class State {idle,ready}; auto state=State::~ready~; std::cout<<(state==State::ready);','1','0|Compile error','Scoped enum values are named through the enum type.','idle|missing')),
 L('concurrency','Threads, joining, and synchronization',
 'C++20 jthread joins automatically when destroyed. A thread that borrows local storage must finish before that storage dies. mutex protects shared operations when every participant follows the same locking protocol; scoped_lock releases the lock on scope exit. atomic supports specified operations without data races, but does not make unrelated state safe. Unsynchronized conflicting access to ordinary memory is undefined behavior, not merely an unpredictable final number.',
 S('int value=0; {std::jthread worker([&]{value=${n};});} std::cout<<~value~;','${n}','0|Undefined behavior','The jthread destructor joins before the final read.','0|1'),
 S('std::atomic<int> value{${n}}; value.~fetch_add~(1); std::cout<<value.load();','${next}','${n}|Compile error','fetch_add atomically increments this integer.','store|exchange'),
 S('std::mutex mutex; {std::~scoped_lock~ guard(mutex);}','Releases the mutex when guard leaves scope','Keeps it locked forever|Launches a thread','The RAII guard releases its lock at the closing brace.','unique_ptr|jthread','How is the mutex released?'),
 S('int value=0; {std::jthread a([&]{~++value~;}); std::jthread b([&]{++value;});}','Undefined behavior: data race','Guaranteed value 2|Guaranteed value 1','Concurrent unsynchronized writes to the same ordinary int form a data race.','value+=1|value=1','What is wrong with the shared counter?'))]
